### Instruction:
List the types of inheritance supported in Java and provide an example of each type. Also, include a piece of erroneous code that demonstrates a common mistake when using one of these types of inheritance.

### Response:
In Java, there are five types of inheritance that are supported: 

1. Single Inheritance: It is when a class inherits from a single parent class.
Example:
```java
class Parent {
    public void display() {
        System.out.println("This is the parent class.");
    }
}

class Child extends Parent {
    public void show() {
        System.out.println("This is the child class.");
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Child obj = new Child();
        obj.display(); // Output: This is the parent class.
        obj.show(); // Output: This is the child class.
    }
}
```

Erroneous code:
```java
class Parent {
    public void display() {
        System.out.println("This is the parent class.");
    }
}

class Child extends Parent {
    public void display() { // Error: Trying to override a final method.
        System.out.println("This is the child class.");
    }
}
```
In the erroneous code, the `display()` method in the `Child` class tries to override the `display()` method in the `Parent` class, but it results in an error because the `display()` method in the `Parent` class is declared as final.

2. Multilevel Inheritance: It is when a class inherits from a derived class, creating a hierarchical inheritance structure.
Example:
```java
class Grandparent {
    public void show() {
        System.out.println("This is the Grandparent class.");
    }
}

class Parent extends Grandparent {
    public void display() {
        System.out.println("This is the Parent class.");
    }
}

class Child extends Parent {
    public void print() {
        System.out.println("This is the Child class.");
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Child obj = new Child();
        obj.show(); // Output: This is the Grandparent class.
        obj.display(); // Output: This is the Parent class.
        obj.print(); // Output: This is the Child class.
    }
}
```

Erroneous code:
```java
class Grandparent {
    public void show() {
        System.out.println("This is the Grandparent class.");
    }
}

class Parent extends Grandparent {
    public void show() { // Error: Trying to override a final method.
        System.out.println("This is the Parent class.");
    }
}

class Child extends Parent {
    public void print() {
        System.out.println("This is the Child class.");
    }
}
```
In the erroneous code, the `show()` method in the `Parent` class tries to override the `show()` method in the `Grandparent` class, but it results in an error because the `show()` method in the `Grandparent` class is declared as final.

3. Hierarchical Inheritance: It is when multiple classes inherit from a single parent class.
Example:
```java
class Parent {
    public void display() {
        System.out.println("This is the parent class.");
    }
}

class Child1 extends Parent {
    public void show1() {
        System.out.println("This is the Child1 class.");
    }
