    public void show2() {
        System.out.println("This is the Child2 class.");
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Child1 obj1 = new Child1();
        Child2 obj2 = new Child2();
        obj1.display(); // Output: This is the parent class.
        obj1.show1(); // Output: This is the Child1 class.
        obj2.display(); // Output: This is the parent class.
        obj2.show2(); // Output: This is the Child2 class.
    }
}
```

Erroneous code:
```java
class Parent {
    public void display() {
        System.out.println("This is the parent class.");
    }
}

class Child1 extends Parent {
    public void display() { // Error: Trying to override a final method.
        System.out.println("This is the Child1 class.");
    }
}

class Child2 extends Parent {
    public void show2() {
        System.out.println("This is the Child2 class.");
    }
}
```
In the erroneous code, the `display()` method in the `Child1` class tries to override the `display()` method in the `Parent` class, but it results in an error because the `display()` method in the `Parent` class is declared as final.

4. Multiple Inheritance (using interfaces): It is when a class implements multiple interfaces.
Example:
```java
interface Interface1 {
    void show1();
}

interface Interface2 {
    void show2();
}

class MyClass implements Interface1, Interface2 {
    public void show1() {
        System.out.println("Implementation of Interface1 method.");
    }

    public void show2() {
        System.out.println("Implementation of Interface2 method.");
    }
