### Instruction:
Explain how constructor chaining works in Java when there are multiple inheritance levels involved, and provide an example code snippet to demonstrate it.

### Response:
Constructor chaining in Java refers to the process of calling one constructor from another constructor within the same class or from a derived class. This mechanism allows code reusability and ensures that all constructors are executed properly.

When there are multiple inheritance levels involved, the constructor chaining follows a top-down approach, where constructors from the base class are called first before moving to the derived class constructors.

Here's an example code snippet to demonstrate constructor chaining with multiple inheritance levels:

```java
class Animal {
    private String name;

    public Animal() {
        this("Unknown");
    }

    public Animal(String name) {
        this.name = name;
        System.out.println("Animal constructor called");
    }

    public String getName() {
        return name;
    }
}

class Dog extends Animal {
    private String breed;

    public Dog() {
        this("Unknown");
    }

    public Dog(String breed) {
        super(); // Call to Animal's default constructor
        this.breed = breed;
        System.out.println("Dog constructor called");
    }

    public String getBreed() {
        return breed;
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Dog dog = new Dog("Labrador");
        System.out.println("Name: " + dog.getName());
        System.out.println("Breed: " + dog.getBreed());
    }
}
```

In this example, we have an `Animal` class with two constructors - a default constructor and a parameterized constructor. The default constructor initializes the `name` variable as "Unknown", while the parameterized constructor allows setting a custom name.

The `Dog` class extends the `Animal` class and introduces an additional variable `breed`. It also has two constructors - a default constructor and a parameterized constructor. The default constructor calls `super()`, which invokes the default constructor of the base class `Animal`. The parameterized constructor sets the `breed` variable and calls the default constructor of the `Dog` class.

