### Instruction:
Explain how constructor chaining works in Java when there are multiple inheritance levels involved, taking into consideration the added constraint that one of the parent classes is an abstract class. Provide an example code snippet to demonstrate the concept, ensuring that the code includes at least one method override in each parent class and child class.

### Response:
Constructor chaining in Java allows us to call one constructor from another constructor within the same class or in its parent class. When there are multiple inheritance levels involved and one of the parent classes is an abstract class, constructor chaining becomes slightly more complex.

Here's an example code snippet that demonstrates constructor chaining with multiple inheritance levels and an abstract parent class:

```java
abstract class Animal {
    private String name;

    public Animal(String name) {
        this.name = name;
    }

    public abstract void makeSound();
}

class Dog extends Animal {
    private int age;

    public Dog(String name, int age) {
        super(name);  // Call parent class constructor
        this.age = age;
    }

    @Override
    public void makeSound() {
        System.out.println("Woof!");
    }

    public void displayInfo() {
        System.out.println("Name: " + super.name);
        System.out.println("Age: " + age);
    }
}

class Puppy extends Dog {
    public Puppy(String name, int age) {
        super(name, age);  // Call parent class constructor
    }

    @Override
    public void makeSound() {
        System.out.println("Bark!");
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Puppy puppy = new Puppy("Max", 1);
        puppy.makeSound();  // Output: Bark!
        puppy.displayInfo();  // Output: Name: Max, Age: 1
    }
}
```

In the above example, we have an abstract class `Animal` with a constructor that takes a `name` parameter. The `Dog` class extends `Animal` and has an additional constructor that takes an `age` parameter. The `Dog` class also overrides the `makeSound()` method from the abstract parent class. The `Puppy` class extends `Dog` and uses the `super()` keyword to call the constructor of the immediate parent class, `Dog`.

In the `main()` method, we create an instance of `Puppy` and call its `makeSound()` and `displayInfo()` methods. The `makeSound()` method is overridden in both `Dog` and `Puppy` classes, demonstrating method overriding in each parent and child class.

The output of the above code will be:

```
Bark!
Name: Max
Age: 1
```

This demonstrates how constructor chaining works in Java with multiple inheritance levels and an abstract parent class.

