Static binding and dynamic binding are two types of binding in Java, and they are related to polymorphism.

Static binding (also known as early binding) is resolved at compile-time. It occurs when the type of the object is determined by the compiler based on the reference type. Method overloading is an example of static binding because the decision on which method to call is made at compile-time based on the parameters.

Here's an example demonstrating static binding:

```java
class Calculator {
    public void add(int a, int b) {
        System.out.println("Sum of two integers: " + (a + b));
    }

    public void add(double a, double b) {
        System.out.println("Sum of two doubles: " + (a + b));
    }
}

public class StaticBindingExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Calculator calculator = new Calculator();

        calculator.add(2, 3);
        calculator.add(2.5, 3.5);
    }
}
```

In this example, the decision on which `add()` method to call is made at compile-time based on the parameter types. The appropriate method is bound statically, and the output is determined at compile-time.

Dynamic binding (also known as late binding) is resolved at runtime. It occurs when the type of the object is determined by the actual object being referred to. Method overriding is an example of dynamic binding because the decision on which method to call is made at runtime based on the actual type of the object.

Here's an example demonstrating dynamic binding:

```java
class Shape {
    public void display() {
        System.out.println("This is a shape");
    }
}

class Rectangle extends Shape {
    @Override
    public void display() {
        System.out.println("This is a rectangle");
    }
}

public class DynamicBindingExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Shape shape = new Rectangle();
        shape.display();
    }
}
```

In this example, the `shape` object is of type `Shape`, but it refers to a `Rectangle` instance. The decision on which `display()` method to call is made at runtime based on the actual type of the object. The appropriate method is bound dynamically, and the output is determined at runtime.

